# Target C-II — every sub-`sqrt(2)` source-pair graph is plane

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proved elementary geometric lemma; proof-only tool for the FREE reflection branch  
**Scope:** every unit-separated planar source; applied to all lower gates in the two-sided FREE repair  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_short_inactive_edge_planarity_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

There is a sharper crossing threshold than the `sqrt(3)` contact-crossing lemma when
**both** source segments are short.

Let `A subset R^2` be unit-separated and form the straight-line graph

\[
P_{<\sqrt2}(A)
:=\{xy:x,y\in A,\ 1\le |x-y|<\sqrt2\}.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{P_{<\sqrt2}(A)\text{ is a plane straight-line graph.}}
\tag{SP.1}

In particular, two independent source segments of lengths below `sqrt(2)` cannot
properly cross.

For the current FREE repair,

\[
t_D<h_*+D^{-1/3},
\qquad
h_*={\sqrt{303}\over52}=0.3347479843\ldots,
\]

and

\[
1+h_*=1.3347479843\ldots<\sqrt2.
\]

Hence, for all sufficiently large `D`, **every lower-gate edge from every reflection
module belongs to one common plane graph** `P_<sqrt(2)(A)`.  The original unit-contact
graph is a subgraph of the same plane graph.  Therefore:

\[
\boxed{
\text{lower-gate edges from distinct modules can meet only at common source vertices;
they cannot pass transversely through one another.}
}
\tag{SP.2}

Combined with the two-sided outer-face localization, the complete collection of
all-inactive lower-gate runs is a plane augmentation of the original outer contact
face.  Different runs may merge, split, or reuse vertices, but any future multi-module
closure can treat those interactions combinatorially in one fixed planar graph rather
than as arbitrary crossing inactive chords.

---

## 1. A short source edge contains no third source point

If a source point `z` lies in the relative interior of the segment `xy`, then

\[
|x-y|=|x-z|+|z-y|\ge2
\]

by unit separation.  Thus no segment of length below `2`, and in particular no edge of
`P_<sqrt(2)(A)`, contains a third source point in its interior.

---

## 2. Two sub-`sqrt(2)` source segments cannot cross

Assume for contradiction that independent edges `ab` and `cd` of
`P_<sqrt(2)(A)` cross properly.  Their four endpoints are then the vertices of a
convex quadrilateral, with `ab` and `cd` as its two diagonals.

The four interior angles of a convex quadrilateral sum to `2pi`, so at least one
interior angle is at least `pi/2`.

Suppose first that this angle is at `c`.  Its adjacent quadrilateral sides join `c` to
`a` and `b`.  Unit separation gives

\[
|c-a|\ge1,
\qquad
|c-b|\ge1.
\]

The diagonal opposite that angle is `ab`.  By the law of cosines,

\[
\begin{aligned}
|a-b|^2
&=|c-a|^2+|c-b|^2
 -2|c-a||c-b|\cos\angle acb\\
&\ge |c-a|^2+|c-b|^2\\
&\ge2.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus `|a-b|>=sqrt(2)`, contradicting the definition of the graph.

If the angle at least `pi/2` occurs at `a` or `b`, the same argument instead forces
`|c-d|>=sqrt(2)`.  The fourth case is symmetric.  Therefore no proper crossing is
possible.

Together with Section 1 this proves SP.1.

The constant is sharp.  The four vertices of a unit square are unit-separated and its
two diagonals have length exactly `sqrt(2)` and cross.  Therefore `<sqrt(2)` cannot be
replaced by `<=sqrt(2)`.

---

## 3. Application to every FREE lower gate

For a lower ancestry edge the two-sided activation theorem gives

\[
1<|z_j-z_{j+1}|
\le1+\Delta_j
\le1+t_D.
\tag{SP.3}
\]

Since

\[
\sqrt2-1-h_*
=0.079465577\ldots>0,
\]

we have

\[
1+t_D<\sqrt2
\]

for all sufficiently large `D`.  Thus every lower gate, independently of which
reflection module generated it, is an edge of the same geometric graph
`P_<sqrt(2)(A)`.

Every unit contact also has length below `sqrt(2)`, so adjoining all lower-gate edges
to the original contact graph preserves planarity.  The outer-face localization theorem
says every all-inactive lower-gate endpoint is already in the original outer contact
face.  Consequently these lower edges subdivide only that face; none can cross the
positive contact core or another lower edge.

This is stronger than proving one selected run is simple.  It is simultaneous over all
lower runs and all reflection modules in the fixed original source.

---

## 4. What this does and does not close

SP.1 removes transverse crossing as a way for many FREE near-unit corridors to coexist.
They must instead coexist by vertex sharing and planar branching in one bounded-degree
geometric proximity graph.  It does not yet bound how many reflection modules can reuse
the same inactive corridor, and therefore does not by itself close C-II.

The next useful multi-module step is to combine this common plane graph with the
ordered canonical reflection normals and the antipodal support localization.  A closure
would follow from a quantitative statement forcing too much vertex sharing, outer-walk
detour, or an active-core gate when many ordered modules are routed through this one
plane near-unit network.

**Disposition:** proved the sharp sub-`sqrt(2)` short-edge planarity lemma and applied it
simultaneously to every FREE lower gate.  C-II and Level I remain open.
